<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_limit.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-limit">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-limit">
  <primary>LIMIT</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>LIMIT</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>LIMIT</refname>
  <refpurpose>cap how many of the working rows are kept</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
LIMIT <replaceable class="parameter">count</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-limit-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>LIMIT</command> keeps at most <replaceable class="parameter">count</replaceable>
   rows and discards the rest. It is a clause of its own, so it can cap the rows anywhere
   in a query. Write it after a <xref linkend="cypher-return"/> or a
   <xref linkend="cypher-with"/>, or straight after a <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>SKIP</command> and its synonym <command>OFFSET</command> discard rows from
   the front instead. They are a separate clause with a page of their own, and either
   clause may be used without the other. See <xref linkend="cypher-skip"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The count is an expression, not only a literal, and it is evaluated once. A negative
   count is an error. A count larger than the number of rows is not: the query returns
   every row it has. A fractional count is rounded to the nearest integer.
  </para>

  <para>
   Without an <xref linkend="cypher-orderby"/>, which rows are kept is not defined. Sort
   first whenever the page has to be reproducible.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>LIMIT</command> cannot open a query and cannot end one. A query still has to
   finish with <command>RETURN</command>, <command>FINISH</command> or a write clause.
  </para>

  <para>
   Written after a projection, the modifiers have to be in the order
   <command>ORDER BY</command>, then <command>SKIP</command> or
   <command>OFFSET</command>, then <command>LIMIT</command>. Only that order folds into
   the projection. In the middle of a query both orders parse and they mean different
   things, because each clause applies to the rows the clause before it produced.
  </para>

  <para>
   A <command>LIMIT</command> that follows a write clause does not truncate the write.
   The write clause processes every row that reached it, and the
   <command>LIMIT</command> caps only the rows the statement carries on with. So
   <literal>MATCH (m:movie) SET m.seen = true RETURN m LIMIT 1</literal> updates every
   film it matched and reports one of them. This holds for
   <literal>LIMIT 0</literal> as well, and for anything else that stops reading a write
   early, such as a join whose other side is empty.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-limit-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">count</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An integer expression that is not negative. At most that many rows are kept. A
      fraction is rounded to the nearest integer, so <literal>LIMIT 1.5</literal> keeps
      two rows.
     </para>
     <para>
      A negative count is refused with
      <literal>LIMIT must not be negative</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-limit-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Take the first rows of a sorted result:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film
LIMIT 2;

      film
----------------
 "Avatar"
 "Blade Runner"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A fractional count rounds, so this keeps two rows as well:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film
LIMIT 1.5;

      film
----------------
 "Avatar"
 "Blade Runner"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   In the middle of a query, <command>LIMIT</command> reduces the rows a later clause has
   to join. The graph holds eighteen films and the second <command>MATCH</command> runs
   against two of them:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
ORDER BY m.year DESC LIMIT 2
MATCH (p:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m)
RETURN m.title AS film, p.name AS director
ORDER BY film;

            film            |      director
----------------------------+--------------------
 "Blade Runner 2049"        | "Denis Villeneuve"
 "The Matrix Resurrections" | "Lana Wachowski"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A count past the end of the result is not an error. There are eighteen films, so
   <literal>LIMIT 100</literal> on the query above returns all eighteen rather than
   failing. A negative count is refused:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) RETURN m.title AS film ORDER BY film LIMIT -1;

ERROR:  LIMIT must not be negative
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-limit-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>LIMIT</command> cannot open a query, so a statement that starts with it is a
   syntax error:
<programlisting>
LIMIT 3;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near "LIMIT"
LINE 1: LIMIT 3;
        ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   And it cannot close one:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) LIMIT 3;

ERROR:  Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The same error reports a modifier written out of order after a projection. Put
   <command>LIMIT</command> before <command>SKIP</command> and the trailing
   <command>SKIP</command> is read as a clause of its own, so the query no longer ends on
   a projection:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) RETURN m.title AS film ORDER BY film LIMIT 3 SKIP 2;

ERROR:  Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   In the middle of a query both orders parse and answer differently.
   <literal>SKIP 2 LIMIT 4</literal> takes rows three to six, while
   <literal>LIMIT 4 SKIP 2</literal> cuts to four rows and then drops two of them,
   leaving two. <xref linkend="cypher-skip"/> shows both.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-limit-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>LIMIT</command> implements the limit clause of ISO/IEC 39075:2024, clause
   16.18, feature GQ13, as part of the order by and page statement of clause 14.9. The
   standard's count is an integer that is not negative, and AgensGraph accepts any
   integer expression in that position, rounding a fraction. The standard sorts an
   unordered table in an order the implementation chooses before taking the first rows,
   which is why a <command>LIMIT</command> without an <command>ORDER BY</command> is not
   reproducible here either.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher writes <command>LIMIT</command> only as a trailing modifier of
   <command>RETURN</command> or <command>WITH</command>. Both of those forms are accepted
   here unchanged, and the standalone clause, which caps the rows after a
   <command>MATCH</command> or anywhere else in a query, is the addition.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

